feat(compiler,tools): the anchors an area guarantees are answerable before the build

An area that binds a `prefab_pool` seats a SUBSET of that pool's members: the
`entry`-role piece at the origin, one carrier per anchor the campaign requires,
and connector fillers drawn from the seed. A `room` or `terminal` member nothing
forces is never seated, and an anchor it declares is not in the built world.
Nothing said so before the build, and the anchors are chosen at the third
authoring step.

`compiler::guarantee` is the one authority for what a binding guarantees, and it
computes it from declarations alone -- no `.nbt`, no placement, no PRNG -- so the
whole verdict is available at validation, on `compiler::seating`'s precedent.
Two readers share it: `delvec prefab anchors` answers per pool from a library
with no campaign at all (the third authoring step has no six documents to hand a
campaign verb), and `validate`/`analyze`/`build` answer per area with `DW0889`.

`DW0889` is advisory. The guaranteed set is sufficient, not necessary: an anchor
carried only by connectors is seated whenever the filler draw picks that
connector, so refusing here would refuse campaigns that build green. `DW0302`,
`DW0142`, `DW0360`, `DW0431` and `DW0447` still refuse at the build. What was
missing was never a refusal -- it was the answer arriving in time.

Two solver rules are named rather than open-coded, because two callers now need
them and must not disagree: `solver::entry_member` and
`solver::required_carriers`. `plan::required_anchors_for_area` becomes
`pub(crate)` for the same reason -- it is the engine's definition of a campaign
reference the layout must honour, and re-deriving it would be the second
enumeration its own doc refuses.

Measured on the shipped content library (`delvec prefab anchors --json`):
`pool/cave-shore`, `pool/stone-keep` and `pool/vertical-keep` guarantee 2 anchor
names of the 10 their members declare; `pool/island` guarantees 11 of 53. Across
the whole library, 17 of 83 names over 42 members in 4 pools.

